WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.402
Doctor shortage by province.

00:00:02.352 --> 00:00:04.659
Where the crisis hits hardest.

00:00:04.659 --> 00:00:09.204
Six point five million Canadians do not have a regular family doctor.

00:00:09.204 --> 00:00:12.147
But the shortage is not evenly distributed.

00:00:12.147 --> 00:00:18.000
New Brunswick has over thirty percent of residents without a family physician — the highest rate in Canada.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.761
British Columbia has over nine hundred thousand people on the GP waitlist.

00:00:22.761 --> 00:00:26.181
Quebec has over one million without a family doctor.

00:00:26.181 --> 00:00:30.613
Atlantic Canada and northern communities face the most severe shortages.

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:38.829
The provincial breakdown shows where the healthcare pipeline fails first — and where MAID eligibility is most likely to be manufactured by system failure.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:41.829
Provincial physician shortage comparison table.

00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:45.068
Data from CIHI and provincial health authorities.

00:00:45.068 --> 00:00:47.511
Provincial physician shortage table.

00:00:47.511 --> 00:00:51.659
New Brunswick: over thirty percent unattached, most severe.

00:00:51.659 --> 00:00:55.465
Nova Scotia: approximately twenty-five percent.

00:00:55.465 --> 00:00:59.568
British Columbia: over nine hundred thousand on waitlist.

00:00:59.568 --> 00:01:02.931
Quebec: over one million without family doctor.

00:01:02.931 --> 00:01:08.045
Ontario: approximately two point five million, largest absolute number.

00:01:08.045 --> 00:01:11.522
Alberta: approximately eight hundred thousand.

00:01:11.522 --> 00:01:15.113
Saskatchewan and Manitoba: moderate shortages.

00:01:15.113 --> 00:01:18.193
Prince Edward Island: proportionally severe.

00:01:18.193 --> 00:01:21.954
Newfoundland and Labrador: severe in rural areas.

00:01:21.954 --> 00:01:24.181
The geographic pipeline.

00:01:24.181 --> 00:01:28.363
The provincial data reveals a geographic dimension to the MAID pipeline.

00:01:28.363 --> 00:01:32.499
Provinces with the highest physician shortages have the highest MAID rates.

00:01:32.499 --> 00:01:36.954
Provinces where immigrants concentrate have the largest absolute shortages.

00:01:36.954 --> 00:01:41.181
Rural and Indigenous communities face the most severe access gaps.

00:01:41.181 --> 00:01:48.602
The healthcare failure that manufactures MAID eligibility is not uniform — it hits specific populations in specific places.

00:01:48.602 --> 00:01:50.454
Atlantic Canada.

00:01:50.454 --> 00:01:52.386
Rural Ontario.

00:01:52.386 --> 00:01:54.329
Northern communities.

00:01:54.329 --> 00:01:56.374
Indigenous reserves.

00:01:56.374 --> 00:02:02.011
These are the populations where the system fails first — and where MAID fills the gap the system created.
